.TH ED 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ed \- line-oriented text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ed
.RB [ \-p
.IR prompt ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ed
is the original Unix text editor.
It reads a file into a buffer of lines, changes the buffer with commands
read from standard input, and writes the file only when told to.
This one is a small implementation with a subset of the usual commands;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
With a
.I file
operand,
.B ed
reads the file into the buffer, prints the number of bytes read unless
.B \-s
is given, and remembers the name for
.B w
and
.BR f .
The last line becomes the current line.
If the file cannot be read,
.B ed
reports the error and exits with status 0 without reading any command,
so it cannot start a new file.
Start
.B ed
with no operand and name the file with
.B w
instead.
Operands after the first are ignored.
With no
.IR file ,
the buffer is empty and has no file name.
.PP
Each line of input is one command: optional addresses, a command letter,
and its argument.
Blanks may surround addresses and precede the command letter, and
trailing blanks on the line are dropped.
A command that fails prints a message on standard error and
.B ed
reads the next command.
There is no
.B ?\&
response and no help command.
.PP
Lines printed by commands, byte counts, and the answers to
.B f
and
.B =
go to standard output.
Error messages, the
.B \-p
prompt, and the
.B Really quit?\&
prompt go to standard error.
.SS Addresses
An address names a line.
.TP
.I n
A decimal line number, counting from 1.
Line 0 stands for the position before the first line.
The
.B a
and
.B r
commands accept it, and most others report an error.
.TP
.B .
The current line.
.TP
.B $
The last line.
.TP
.BI \(aq x
The line marked with the lowercase letter
.I x
by the
.B k
command.
.TP
.BI / text /
The first line from the current line to the last that contains
.IR text .
The search starts at the current line itself, so repeating it finds the
same line, and it does not wrap around.
The text is matched literally, not as a regular expression, and cannot
contain a slash.
If the closing slash is missing, the rest of the line is the text.
An empty
.I text
repeats the last string searched for by an address or by
.BR s .
.PP
Addresses can be added and subtracted, as in
.BR $\-2 ,
.BR .+1 ,
and
.BR /text/+3 .
Blanks are allowed around operands and operators.
A leading
.B +
or
.B \-
is not an address.
Whether the result lies in the buffer is checked by the command that
uses it.
.PP
A range is two addresses separated by a comma.
A missing first address means 1 and a missing second means
.BR $ ,
so a lone comma means the whole buffer.
There are no semicolon ranges.
Both addresses are evaluated from the current line, so a search in the
second does not start after the first.
Given one address, a command that takes a range applies to that line
alone.
.SS Text input
The
.BR a ,
.BR c ,
and
.B i
commands read lines of text and add them to the buffer.
Input ends at a line holding only a period, which is not added, so a
line of text cannot be a lone period.
It also ends at the end of input, and in a console at Ctrl\-D on an empty
line or at Ctrl\-C, which discards the line being typed.
The last line added becomes the current line.
The prompt is not shown in this mode.
.PP
The insertion point is checked only when the first line of text
arrives.
If it is out of range,
.B ed
reports
.BR "inserting at bad line number" ,
discards that line, and reads the lines after it as commands.
.SS Commands
In the forms below, omitting an address in brackets uses the current line
unless noted.
The commands
.BR a ,
.BR c ,
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR l ,
.BR p ,
and
.B =
ignore any text after the command letter.
The commands
.BR f ,
.BR r ,
and
.B w
need a space, not a tab, before their argument.
Any other command letter is reported as
.BR "unimplemented command" .
.TP
\fR[\fIaddr\fR]\fBa\fR
Read text and insert it after line
.IR addr .
With a range, the first address is used.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBc\fR
Delete the lines, then read text and insert it where they were.
If the range is invalid, nothing is deleted, but
.B ed
still reads text and inserts it before the first address.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBd\fR
Delete the lines.
If the current line was deleted, the line after them becomes current, or
the new last line when none follows, or line 0 when the buffer is empty.
Otherwise the same line stays current.
.TP
\fBf\fR [\fIname\fR]
With
.IR name ,
make it the remembered file name, which
.B w
uses when given none.
The name is the rest of the line after the blanks, so it may contain
spaces.
Without
.IR name ,
print the current file name in double quotes, or
.B No file name
if there is none.
.TP
\fR[\fIaddr\fR]\fBi\fR
Read text and insert it before line
.IR addr .
With a range, the first address is used.
In an empty buffer with no address, the text becomes line 1.
.TP
\fR[\fIaddr\fR]\fBk\fIx\fR
Mark a line with the lowercase letter
.IR x .
With a range, the last address is used.
The address is not checked.
A mark is a line number, so it does not follow its line when lines are
added or deleted.
An unset mark is line 0.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBl\fR
Print the lines unambiguously.
A control character appears as
.B ^
and a character, such as
.B ^I
for a tab,
.B ^M
for a carriage return, and
.B ^[
for escape.
Delete appears as
.BR ^? .
A byte above 127 appears as
.B M\-
followed by the byte with its high bit cleared, shown the same way, so
UTF-8 text appears as
.B M\-
sequences.
Each line ends with
.BR $ ,
long lines are not folded, and a backslash or dollar sign in the text
appears as itself.
The last line printed becomes current.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBp\fR
Print the lines exactly as stored.
A final line that has no newline is printed without one.
The last line printed becomes current.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
No address or argument is allowed.
If the buffer has changed since it was read or last written by
.B w
without an address,
.B ed
prints
.B Really quit?\&
and reads a line.
It quits if the first non-blank character of that line is
.B y
or
.BR Y ,
and otherwise continues.
End of input at that prompt quits, and Ctrl\-C continues.
The commands
.BR a ,
.BR c ,
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR r ,
and a
.B s
that replaces something change the buffer.
End of input or Ctrl\-C at the ordinary command prompt exits without the
check, losing any changes.
.TP
\fR[\fIaddr\fR]\fBr \fIfile\fR
Read
.I file
and insert its lines after line
.IR addr ,
which is the first address of a range and defaults to the last line.
Address 0 inserts before the first line.
The byte count is printed unless
.B \-s
is given.
The last line read becomes current.
The file name is the rest of the line after the blanks.
It is required: the remembered file name is not used, and there is no
form that runs a command.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBs/\fIold\fB/\fInew\fB/\fR[\fIflags\fR]
Replace the first occurrence of
.I old
in each line of the range by
.IR new .
Any character except a space or tab may replace the slashes.
The strings are literal: there are no regular expressions, and
.BR & ,
.BR \e1 ,
and backslash have no special meaning.
The delimiter cannot appear in either string.
An empty
.I old
repeats the last string searched for by an address or by
.BR s ,
and an empty
.I new
deletes.
The final delimiter may be omitted when there are no flags, and
trailing blanks on the command line are dropped, so end
.I new
with the delimiter to keep trailing blanks.
The flags are
.BR g ,
to replace every occurrence, left to right, without rescanning the
replacement, and
.BR p ,
to print each changed line.
It is an error if no line changed.
The current line does not change, except that with
.B p
the last line printed becomes current.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]\fBw\fR [\fIfile\fR]
Write the lines to
.IR file ,
or when it is omitted to the remembered file name, replacing the
contents of the file.
With no address, the whole buffer is written and counts as saved, even if
the write fails, so a following
.B q
quits without asking.
With an address, only those lines are written and the saved state is
unchanged.
The name given does not become the remembered name.
The byte count is printed unless
.B \-s
is given.
An empty buffer cannot be written.
There is no
.B wq
command.
.TP
\fBz\fR[\fB.\fR|\fB\-\fR]
Print 22 lines: with no argument, from the current line;
with
.BR . ,
from 11 lines before it to 10 after;
with
.BR \- ,
ending at it.
The last line printed becomes current.
An address, and any other text after
.BR z ,
is ignored.
All 22 lines must lie inside the buffer, or the command fails.
.TP
\fR[\fIaddr\fR]\fB=\fR
Print the line number that
.I addr
gives, which is the first address of a range and defaults to the current
line.
.B $=
prints the number of lines.
.TP
.B \-
Make the previous line current and print it.
Text after it is ignored, so
.B \-3
also goes back one line.
.TP
\fR[\fIrange\fR]
A line with no command letter prints the last line of the range, which
becomes current.
With no address, it prints the line after the current line, which
becomes current.
.SH OPTIONS
.B ed
accepts options before or after the file operand, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The last of repeated options wins.
An argument may follow
.B \-p
attached or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BI "\-p " prompt
Show
.I prompt
on standard error before reading each command, even when input is not a
console.
It is not shown in text input mode.
Backslash sequences in it are expanded as in the shell prompts described
in
.BR sh (1),
such as
.B \eu
for the user name,
.B \e$
for a dollar sign, or a number sign when elevated, and
.B \et
for the time.
.B \ew
and
.BR \eW ,
the current directory, work only when standard input is not a console:
in a console they crash
.BR ed .
Parameters and commands in it are not expanded.
The default is no prompt.
.TP
.B \-s
Do not print the byte counts for reading and writing files.
Error messages and all other output are unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B ed
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
Quoted arguments are expanded too, so a prompt such as
.B *
given to
.B \-p
may become file names.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal.
When it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
files that
.B w
creates are read-only, so a later
.B w
cannot replace them.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The console width that the line editor wraps and redraws lines to.
The default is the width of the console window of standard output or
standard error, or 80 when neither is a console.
.TP
.B HOME
The directory that
.B \ew
and
.B \eW
in the
.B \-p
prompt show as
.BR ~ .
When it is unset or empty,
.B ed
uses the user's profile directory.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides how the line editor handles text.
When its value is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes, and console output is written without code page
conversion.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, and single
bytes when it is not.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the
.I file
operand ends the options, so that a later argument such as
.B \-s
is an ignored extra operand.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every normal end: the
.B q
command, end of input, Ctrl\-C or Ctrl\-D at the command prompt, or
.BR \-\-help .
Errors in commands, and a file operand that cannot be read, do not change
the status.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or a missing option argument, with a usage message on
standard error, or out of memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Replace every
.B old
with
.B new
in a file, from a script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ed \-s config.h <<\(aqEOF\(aq
,s/old/new/g
w
q
EOF
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print lines 10 to 20 of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq10,20p\en\(aq | ed \-s file.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Insert a line at the top of a file, and save it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq0a\en#include <stdio.h>\en.\enw\enq\en\(aq | ed \-s main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa\enhello\en.\enw hello.txt\enq\en\(aq | ed \-s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the number of lines in a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq$=\en\(aq | ed \-s file.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
This is the
.B ed
of busybox, derived from a small 2002 implementation, not GNU
.BR ed .
It differs from POSIX
.B ed
in many ways:
.IP \(bu 2
Searches and substitutions are literal text.
There are no regular expressions, no backward search, and no semicolon
ranges.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the commands above exist.
There is no undo, no
.BR g ,
.BR v ,
.BR j ,
.BR m ,
.BR t ,
.BR u ,
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR n ,
.BR h ,
.BR H ,
.BR P ,
.BR Q ,
or
.BR ! ,
and no suffix such as
.B p
or
.B n
after a command other than
.BR s .
.IP \(bu 2
Errors are messages on standard error, and the exit status does not
report them.
.IP \(bu 2
Commands that take one address accept a range and use its first address,
except
.BR k ,
which uses the last.
.B =
without an address prints the current line number rather than the number
of lines.
.IP \(bu 2
A successful
.B r
does not set the remembered file name, but a failed
.B r
sets it when there is none.
.B w
never sets it.
.PP
The whole buffer is held in memory.
Files are read and written in binary mode, so
.B ed
never translates line endings or character sets.
A line from a file with CR LF endings keeps its carriage return, which
.B p
sends to the output,
.B l
shows as
.BR ^M ,
and
.B s
patterns can match.
.PP
When standard input is not a console, a carriage return before a newline
is dropped from each line read.
A lone period ended by CR LF is still not recognized as the end of text
input, so the rest of the input becomes text.
Scripts for
.B ed
need LF line endings, as made by
.BR dos2unix (1).
.PP
In a console, lines are edited with the line editor of
.BR sh (1),
using the keys in its Line editing section, but with no history, no
completion, and no vi mode.
Up, Down, and Tab do nothing, so a tab cannot be typed.
Typing into the history search that Ctrl\-R starts crashes
.BR ed ,
losing the buffer.
Ctrl\-Z changes backslashes in the line to slashes.
Ctrl\-C, or Ctrl\-D on an empty line, at the command prompt exits
.BR ed ,
discarding unsaved changes.
.PP
A command holds at most 1021 characters, and a line of text 1022, not
counting the newline.
A console ignores further typing.
From a pipe or file, the excess is read as a separate command or line of
text, and pieces of text rejoin when written.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and either kind of slash.
The file operand and
.B r
also accept the device names listed in
.BR busybox (1),
such as
.IR /dev/null ,
but
.B w
does not: it treats
.I /dev/null
as an ordinary path, which usually fails.
.PP
On a console, escape sequences in the text of a line take effect when
.B p
prints it, so use
.B l
to look at them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR vim (1)
